{#- ===========================================================================
    Cognisys - Full Report
    PlexTrac PDF export template (.j2)

    Reproduces "New UPD-Full Report Template Cognisys.docx" as HTML + CSS so
    PlexTrac can export straight to PDF instead of going via Word.

    - Data model and macros are ported from the PlexTrac "Jinja Framework v1.5.1"
      that ships inside the Word template, so the same PlexTrac fields drive both.
    - Page geometry, fonts, colours and spacing are taken from the .docx itself
      (see README.md for the measurement table).

    DO NOT UPLOAD THIS FILE DIRECTLY. Run `python build/build.py` and upload
    dist/cognisys-full-report.j2, which has the fonts and logos inlined.
=========================================================================== -#}

{#- =========================== CONFIGURATION =============================== -#}
{%- set REPORT_TITLE_SUFFIX = " Report" -%}   {#- appended after the report title on the cover -#}
{%- set REPORT_VERSION      = "1.0" -%}
{%- set CONFIDENTIALITY     = "Commercial in Confidence" -%}   {#- footer, left -#}
{%- set SHOW_EXHIBITS       = false -%}       {#- the .docx does not print exhibits; set true to add a Screenshots block -#}
{%- set PAGE_BREAK_PER_H1   = true  -%}       {#- true = every top-level section starts on a new page -#}
{%- set PAGE_BREAK_PER_FINDING = true  -%}    {#- true = every finding starts on a new page -#}
{%- set EXCLUDE_FINDING_TITLES = [] -%}       {#- e.g. ["Port Scans"]; see FINDINGS SETUP below -#}
{%- set MISSING_NARRATIVE_NOTE = true -%}     {#- print "NOTE: ... is missing" like the .docx does -#}
{%- set DEBUG_CONTEXT       = false -%}       {#- true = dump the render context at the front of the PDF -#}

{#- ===================== CONTEXT GUARDS ===================================
    PlexTrac's PDF pipeline does not necessarily hand over the same object
    shape as the Word one, so each container is resolved from every name it is
    known to arrive under, and everything falls back to an empty container -
    a template that raises renders nothing at all.
=========================================================================== -#}
{#- Recorded before the guards below shadow these names, for DEBUG_CONTEXT. -#}
{%- set _had = namespace(REPORT_INFO = REPORT_INFO is defined,
                         CLIENT_INFO = CLIENT_INFO is defined,
                         FINDINGS = FINDINGS is defined,
                         FINDING_SUMMARY = FINDING_SUMMARY is defined) -%}

{%- set REPORT_INFO = REPORT_INFO if REPORT_INFO is defined and REPORT_INFO
                 else (report if report is defined and report
                 else (REPORT if REPORT is defined and REPORT else {})) -%}
{%- set CLIENT_INFO = CLIENT_INFO if CLIENT_INFO is defined and CLIENT_INFO
                 else (client if client is defined and client
                 else (CLIENT if CLIENT is defined and CLIENT else {})) -%}
{%- set FINDINGS = FINDINGS if FINDINGS is defined and FINDINGS
              else (findings if findings is defined and findings else []) -%}
{%- set FINDING_SUMMARY = FINDING_SUMMARY if FINDING_SUMMARY is defined and FINDING_SUMMARY
                     else (finding_summary if finding_summary is defined and finding_summary else {}) -%}

{#- Narrative sections. The Word context exposes them as
    REPORT_INFO.executive_summary.custom_fields; the underlying PlexTrac report
    object calls the same thing exec_summary, and some contexts hand over the
    list directly. Resolve once, here, and let the macros read the result. -#}
{%- set _ns = namespace(narratives=[]) -%}
{%- for _src in [REPORT_INFO.executive_summary, REPORT_INFO.exec_summary, REPORT_INFO] -%}
  {%- if not _ns.narratives and _src -%}
    {%- if _src.custom_fields -%}
      {%- set _ns.narratives = _src.custom_fields -%}
    {%- elif _src is sequence and _src is not mapping and _src is not string -%}
      {%- set _ns.narratives = _src -%}
    {%- endif -%}
  {%- endif -%}
{%- endfor -%}
{%- if not _ns.narratives and NARRATIVES is defined and NARRATIVES -%}
  {%- set _ns.narratives = NARRATIVES -%}
{%- endif -%}
{%- set narratives = _ns.narratives -%}

{#- ============================== NAMESPACES =============================== -#}
{%- set ss = namespace() -%}   {#- strings  -#}
{%- set bb = namespace() -%}   {#- booleans -#}
{%- set nn = namespace() -%}   {#- integers -#}
{%- set ff = namespace() -%}   {#- floats   -#}

{#- ============================ TEXT HELPERS =============================== -#}

{#- Emit a PlexTrac field. Rich-text fields already contain HTML and are passed
    through; plain strings are escaped and wrapped in a paragraph. Same
    plain/rich heuristic the Word framework uses (striptags length compare). -#}
{%- macro rich(value, tag="p") -%}
  {%- set ss.rich = value|string|trim -%}
  {%- if ss.rich != "" and ss.rich != "None" -%}
    {%- if ss.rich|striptags|length == ss.rich|length -%}
      <{{ tag }} class="plain">{{ ss.rich|e }}</{{ tag }}>
    {%- else -%}
      {{ ss.rich|safe }}
    {%- endif -%}
  {%- endif -%}
{%- endmacro -%}

{#- Normalise a PlexTrac field label into its lookup key. -#}
{%- macro _key(key) -%}
  {{- (key|default("", true)|string|replace(" ", "_")|replace(")", "")|replace("(", "")|replace("/", "")|replace(":", ""))|lower -}}
{%- endmacro -%}

{#- Find a value on a PlexTrac object and leave it in ss.value (empty string if
    not found). This prints nothing - call it, then read ss.value.

    PlexTrac exposes custom fields in three different shapes depending on which
    export path built the context, so all three are tried in turn:
      1. a slug straight on the object            obj.report_title
      2. obj.fields as a dict keyed by slug       obj.fields.report_title.value
      3. a list of {label, value} under any of obj.fields / obj.custom_fields
         / obj.custom_field (singular - what the raw report object uses)
    Shape 3 is the one the Word context does not use and the PDF context does. -#}
{%- macro _lookup(obj, key) -%}
  {%- set ss.lk = _key(key)|string -%}
  {%- set ss.value = "" -%}
  {%- if obj -%}
    {%- if obj[ss.lk] -%}{%- set ss.value = obj[ss.lk] -%}{%- endif -%}

    {%- if not ss.value and obj.fields is mapping -%}
      {%- if obj.fields[ss.lk] and obj.fields[ss.lk].value -%}
        {%- set ss.value = obj.fields[ss.lk].value -%}
      {%- endif -%}
    {%- endif -%}

    {%- for _bag in [obj.fields, obj.custom_fields, obj.custom_field] -%}
      {%- if not ss.value and _bag and _bag is sequence and _bag is not mapping and _bag is not string -%}
        {%- for _f in _bag -%}
          {%- if not ss.value and _key(_f.label if _f.label else _f.key)|string == ss.lk -%}
            {%- set ss.value = _f.value if _f.value else (_f.text if _f.text else "") -%}
          {%- endif -%}
        {%- endfor -%}
      {%- endif -%}
    {%- endfor -%}
  {%- endif -%}
{%- endmacro -%}

{#- The label of a narrative/custom field, whatever the context calls it. -#}
{%- macro _label(field) -%}
  {{- (field.label if field.label else (field.title if field.title else (field.name if field.name else "")))|string -}}
{%- endmacro -%}

{#- The body text of a narrative/custom field, whatever the context calls it. -#}
{%- macro _text(field) -%}
  {{- (field.text if field.text else (field.value if field.value else (field.content if field.content else "")))|string|safe -}}
{%- endmacro -%}

{#- ============================ CLIENT MACROS ============================== -#}
{%- macro print_client(key, default="No Value") -%}
  {{- _lookup(CLIENT_INFO, key) -}}
  {{- (ss.value if ss.value else default)|string|trim|e -}}
{%- endmacro -%}

{#- True when the field carries an actual value - a field left blank in
    PlexTrac can still come through as whitespace, so trim before testing. -#}
{%- macro check_client(key) -%}
  {{- _lookup(CLIENT_INFO, key) -}}
  {%- if ss.value|string|trim -%}True{%- endif -%}
{%- endmacro -%}

{#- ============================ REPORT MACROS ============================== -#}
{%- macro print_report(key, default=none) -%}
  {{- _lookup(REPORT_INFO, key) -}}
  {%- if ss.value -%}
    {{- ss.value|string|trim|e -}}
  {%- else -%}
    {{- (default if default is not none else ("No Value was input for: " ~ key))|e -}}
  {%- endif -%}
{%- endmacro -%}

{#- True when the field carries an actual value - see check_client. -#}
{%- macro check_report(key) -%}
  {{- _lookup(REPORT_INFO, key) -}}
  {%- if ss.value|string|trim -%}True{%- endif -%}
{%- endmacro -%}

{#- =========================== NARRATIVE MACROS ============================ -#}
{#- Matches on the narrative label, ignoring case, surrounding whitespace and a
    trailing "s" - PlexTrac reports in the wild have both "Limitation" and
    "Limitations", and losing a whole section to a plural is not worth it. -#}
{%- macro _label_matches(field, title) -%}
  {%- set ss.a = _label(field)|string|lower|trim -%}
  {%- set ss.b = title|string|lower|trim -%}
  {%- if ss.a == ss.b or ss.a == ss.b ~ "s" or ss.a ~ "s" == ss.b -%}True{%- endif -%}
{%- endmacro -%}

{%- macro display_narrative(title, error=true) -%}
  {%- set bb.display = false -%}
  {%- for field in narratives -%}
    {%- if _label_matches(field, title)|trim -%}
      {%- set bb.display = true -%}
      {{ rich(_text(field)) }}
    {%- endif -%}
  {%- endfor -%}
  {%- if error and MISSING_NARRATIVE_NOTE and bb.display == false -%}
    <p class="tpl-note">NOTE: Report Narrative {{ title|e }} is missing from your Plextrac report.</p>
  {%- endif -%}
{%- endmacro -%}

{#- type: exact | inset | notin | prefix | tag -#}
{%- macro check_narratives(key, type='exact') -%}
  {%- for field in narratives -%}
    {%- set ss.lbl = _label(field)|string|lower|trim -%}
    {%- if type == "exact" and _label_matches(field, key)|trim -%}True
    {%- elif type == "inset" and key|lower in ss.lbl -%}True
    {%- elif type == "notin" and key|lower not in ss.lbl -%}True
    {%- elif type == "prefix" and ss.lbl.startswith(key|lower) -%}True
    {%- elif type == "tag" and field.tags and key in field.tags -%}True
    {%- endif -%}
  {%- endfor -%}
{%- endmacro -%}

{#- type: inset | notin | startswith | tag -#}
{%- macro loop_narratives(key, type='tag', label='on') -%}
  {%- set bb.default = true -%}
  {%- if type in ['inset', 'notin', 'startswith', 'tag'] -%}
    {%- for field in narratives -%}
      {%- set ss.lbl = _label(field)|string|lower|trim -%}
      {%- set bb.match = false -%}
      {%- if type == "inset" and key|lower in ss.lbl -%}{%- set bb.match = true -%}
      {%- elif type == "notin" and key|lower not in ss.lbl -%}{%- set bb.match = true -%}
      {%- elif type == "startswith" and ss.lbl.startswith(key|lower) -%}{%- set bb.match = true -%}
      {%- elif type == "tag" and field.tags and key in field.tags -%}{%- set bb.match = true -%}
      {%- endif -%}
      {%- if bb.match -%}
        {%- set bb.default = false -%}
        {%- if label == "on" %}<h2>{{ _label(field)|e }}</h2>{% endif -%}
        {{ rich(_text(field)) }}
      {%- endif -%}
    {%- endfor -%}
    {%- if bb.default -%}
      <p class="tpl-note">ERROR: No Narratives matched '{{ key|e }}' ({{ type|e }}).</p>
    {%- endif -%}
  {%- else -%}
    <p class="tpl-note">DEVELOPER ERROR: An incorrect parameter was given for type.</p>
  {%- endif -%}
{%- endmacro -%}

{#- =========================== FINDING MACROS ============================== -#}
{%- macro _finding_value(f, key) -%}
  {%- set ss.value = "" -%}
  {#- business_impact is a built-in PlexTrac field that is never populated -#}
  {%- if key not in ["business_impact"] and f[key] -%}
    {%- if key in ["recommendations", "references"] and f[key] is not string
           and f[key] is sequence and f[key] is not mapping -%}
      {%- set ss.value = f[key][0] -%}
    {%- else -%}
      {%- set ss.value = f[key] -%}
    {%- endif -%}
  {%- endif -%}
  {#- fall back to the custom-field bags, in any of their shapes -#}
  {%- if not ss.value -%}
    {{- _lookup(f, key) -}}
  {%- endif -%}
{%- endmacro -%}

{%- macro print_finding(f, key, default="") -%}
  {{- _finding_value(f, key) -}}
  {{ rich(ss.value if ss.value else default) }}
{%- endmacro -%}

{%- macro check_finding(f, key) -%}
  {{- _finding_value(f, key) -}}
  {%- if ss.value -%}True{%- endif -%}
{%- endmacro -%}

{#- option: full | score_only -#}
{%- macro print_cvss(f, option="full", keys=["CVSS4", "CVSS3_1", "CVSS3", "CVSS2"]) -%}
  {%- set bb.default = true -%}
  {%- for key in keys -%}
    {%- if f.risk_score and f.risk_score[key] -%}
      {%- set bb.default = false -%}
      {%- if option == "score_only" -%}
        {{ "%.1f"|format(f.risk_score[key].overall) }}
      {%- else -%}
        {{ key|replace("_", ".") }}: {{ "%.1f"|format(f.risk_score[key].overall) }} ({{ f.risk_score[key].vector|e }})
      {%- endif -%}
    {%- endif -%}
  {%- endfor -%}
  {%- if bb.default -%}
    {%- if option == "score_only" -%}0.0{%- else -%}No CVSS score was input for this Finding.{%- endif -%}
  {%- endif -%}
{%- endmacro -%}

{#- Exhibit images. PlexTrac hands exhibits over differently depending on the
    export path, so try the shapes we know about and fall back to nothing.
    Verify this against a real export before relying on it - see README.md. -#}
{%- macro exhibit_src(exhibit) -%}
  {%- if exhibit.data -%}data:{{ exhibit.type|default("image/png", true) }};base64,{{ exhibit.data }}
  {%- elif exhibit.base64 -%}data:{{ exhibit.type|default("image/png", true) }};base64,{{ exhibit.base64 }}
  {%- elif exhibit.url -%}{{ exhibit.url|e }}
  {%- elif exhibit.path -%}{{ exhibit.path|e }}
  {%- endif -%}
{%- endmacro -%}

{%- macro print_screenshots(f, captions=true) -%}
  {%- if f.exhibits -%}
    {%- for exhibit in f.exhibits -%}
      {%- set ss.src = exhibit_src(exhibit)|trim -%}
      {%- if ss.src -%}
        <figure class="exhibit">
          <img src="{{ ss.src }}" alt="{{ exhibit.caption|default('', true)|e }}">
          {%- if captions and exhibit.caption %}<figcaption>{{ exhibit.caption|e }}</figcaption>{% endif -%}
        </figure>
      {%- endif -%}
    {%- endfor -%}
  {%- endif -%}
{%- endmacro -%}

{#- ============================ PROCEDURE MACROS =========================== -#}
{%- macro print_procedure(procedure, label, show_label=false, default='--') -%}
  {%- set bb.default = true -%}
  {%- if procedure.customFields -%}
    {%- for field in procedure.customFields -%}
      {%- if label == field.label -%}
        {%- set bb.default = false -%}
        {%- if show_label %}<h3>{{ field.label|e }}</h3>{% endif -%}
        {{ rich(field.text) }}
      {%- endif -%}
    {%- endfor -%}
  {%- endif -%}
  {%- if bb.default -%}{{ default|e }}{%- endif -%}
{%- endmacro -%}

{%- macro loop_mitre(procedure, kind, key, default='--') -%}
  {%- set bb.default = true -%}
  {%- for item in procedure.tagsList -%}
    {%- if item.type == kind -%}
      {%- set bb.default = false -%}
      {{ item.shortName|e }} - {{ item[key]|e }}<br>
    {%- endif -%}
  {%- endfor -%}
  {%- if bb.default -%}{{ default|e }}{%- endif -%}
{%- endmacro -%}
{%- macro loop_tactics(procedure, key, default='--') -%}{{ loop_mitre(procedure, "tactics", key, default) }}{%- endmacro -%}
{%- macro loop_techniques(procedure, key, default='--') -%}{{ loop_mitre(procedure, "techniques", key, default) }}{%- endmacro -%}

{#- =============================== COUNTS ================================== -#}
{%- macro count(keys=[], values=[], list=[]) -%}
  {%- set nn.total_count = 0 -%}
  {%- for key in keys -%}
    {%- set ss.value = values[loop.index0] -%}
    {%- if key not in ["tags"] -%}
      {%- set nn.total_count = nn.total_count + list|selectattr(key, "equalto", ss.value)|list|length -%}
    {%- else -%}
      {%- for f in list if f.tags and ss.value in f[key] -%}
        {%- set nn.total_count = nn.total_count + 1 -%}
      {%- endfor -%}
    {%- endif -%}
  {%- endfor -%}
  {{- nn.total_count -}}
{%- endmacro -%}

{#- ============================ TEXT FORMATTING ============================ -#}
{%- set NUMBER_WORDS = ["zero", "one", "two", "three", "four", "five", "six", "seven", "eight", "nine", "ten",
  "eleven", "twelve", "thirteen", "fourteen", "fifteen", "sixteen", "seventeen", "eighteen", "nineteen", "twenty",
  "twenty-one", "twenty-two", "twenty-three", "twenty-four", "twenty-five", "twenty-six", "twenty-seven",
  "twenty-eight", "twenty-nine", "thirty", "thirty-one", "thirty-two", "thirty-three", "thirty-four", "thirty-five",
  "thirty-six", "thirty-seven", "thirty-eight", "thirty-nine", "forty", "forty-one", "forty-two", "forty-three",
  "forty-four", "forty-five", "forty-six", "forty-seven", "forty-eight", "forty-nine", "fifty", "fifty-one",
  "fifty-two", "fifty-three", "fifty-four", "fifty-five", "fifty-six", "fifty-seven", "fifty-eight", "fifty-nine",
  "sixty", "sixty-one", "sixty-two", "sixty-three", "sixty-four", "sixty-five", "sixty-six", "sixty-seven",
  "sixty-eight", "sixty-nine", "seventy", "seventy-one", "seventy-two", "seventy-three", "seventy-four",
  "seventy-five", "seventy-six", "seventy-seven", "seventy-eight", "seventy-nine", "eighty", "eighty-one",
  "eighty-two", "eighty-three", "eighty-four", "eighty-five", "eighty-six", "eighty-seven", "eighty-eight",
  "eighty-nine", "ninety", "ninety-one", "ninety-two", "ninety-three", "ninety-four", "ninety-five", "ninety-six",
  "ninety-seven", "ninety-eight", "ninety-nine", "one-hundred"] -%}

{%- macro format_number(num) -%}
  {%- set nn.n = num|string|striptags|int -%}
  {%- if nn.n < NUMBER_WORDS|length -%}{{ NUMBER_WORDS[nn.n] }}{%- else -%}{{ nn.n }}{%- endif -%}
{%- endmacro -%}

{#- ============================ DATE FORMATTING ============================ -#}
{%- set MONTHS = ["January", "February", "March", "April", "May", "June", "July",
                  "August", "September", "October", "November", "December"] -%}

{#- Milliseconds since the epoch -> "DD Month YYYY". Civil-from-days algorithm,
    so it is exact for every date (the Word framework approximated). -#}
{%- macro epoch_to_date(time) -%}
  {%- set nn.days = (time|int / 86400000)|int + 719468 -%}
  {%- set nn.era = (nn.days if nn.days >= 0 else nn.days - 146096) // 146097 -%}
  {%- set nn.doe = nn.days - nn.era * 146097 -%}
  {%- set nn.yoe = (nn.doe - nn.doe // 1460 + nn.doe // 36524 - nn.doe // 146096) // 365 -%}
  {%- set nn.doy = nn.doe - (365 * nn.yoe + nn.yoe // 4 - nn.yoe // 100) -%}
  {%- set nn.mp = (5 * nn.doy + 2) // 153 -%}
  {%- set nn.day = nn.doy - (153 * nn.mp + 2) // 5 + 1 -%}
  {%- set nn.month = nn.mp + 3 if nn.mp < 10 else nn.mp - 9 -%}
  {%- set nn.year = nn.yoe + nn.era * 400 + (1 if nn.month <= 2 else 0) -%}
  {{- nn.day }} {{ MONTHS[nn.month - 1] }} {{ nn.year -}}
{%- endmacro -%}

{#- ============================== MULTISCOPE =============================== -#}
{%- set scope_tags  = ['external', 'internal'] -%}
{%- set scope_names = ['External Penetration Test', 'Internal Penetration Test'] -%}
{%- set present_scopes = [] -%}
{%- if REPORT_INFO.tags -%}
  {%- for tag in REPORT_INFO.tags -%}
    {%- if tag in scope_tags and tag not in present_scopes -%}
      {%- set _ = present_scopes.append(tag) -%}
    {%- endif -%}
  {%- endfor -%}
{%- endif -%}

{%- macro set_scope(scope) -%}
  {%- set nn.scope = scope_tags.index(scope) -%}
  {%- set ss.scope_name = scope_names[nn.scope] -%}
  {%- set ss.scope_tag = scope_tags[nn.scope] -%}
{%- endmacro -%}

{#- ==================== BUILT-IN REFERENCES AND VARIABLES ================== -#}

{#- Export date, formatted "DD Month YYYY" like the Word template. -#}
{%- set ss.export_date = "" -%}
{%- set ss.raw_date = (REPORT_INFO.export_datetime_us if REPORT_INFO.export_datetime_us
                  else (REPORT_INFO.export_date if REPORT_INFO.export_date else ""))|string -%}
{%- if ss.raw_date -%}
  {%- set date_array = ss.raw_date.split(" ")[0].split("-") -%}
  {%- if date_array|length == 3 and date_array[0]|int > 0 and date_array[0]|int < 13 -%}
    {%- set ss.export_date = date_array[1] ~ " " ~ MONTHS[date_array[0]|int - 1] ~ " " ~ date_array[2]|replace(",", "") -%}
  {%- else -%}
    {#- unknown format: print it as supplied rather than mangling it -#}
    {%- set ss.export_date = ss.raw_date -%}
  {%- endif -%}
{%- endif -%}
{%- set export_date = ss.export_date -%}

{%- set ss.start_date = REPORT_INFO.start_date if REPORT_INFO.start_date else "No value for Start Date" -%}
{%- set ss.end_date   = REPORT_INFO.end_date   if REPORT_INFO.end_date   else "No value for End Date" -%}

{%- set severities = ["Critical", "High", "Medium", "Low", "Informational"] -%}
{%- set colors     = ["000000", "FF0000", "FFC000", "00B0F0", "00B050"] -%}

{%- set nn.total    = FINDING_SUMMARY.totals.total_reported if FINDING_SUMMARY else 0 -%}
{%- set nn.critical = FINDING_SUMMARY.critical.total       if FINDING_SUMMARY else 0 -%}
{%- set nn.high     = FINDING_SUMMARY.high.total           if FINDING_SUMMARY else 0 -%}
{%- set nn.medium   = FINDING_SUMMARY.medium.total         if FINDING_SUMMARY else 0 -%}
{%- set nn.low      = FINDING_SUMMARY.low.total            if FINDING_SUMMARY else 0 -%}
{%- set nn.info     = FINDING_SUMMARY.informational.total  if FINDING_SUMMARY else 0 -%}

{#- ========================== FINDINGS SETUP ===============================
    The Word template's filter was `if f.title != 'Port Scans' or f.title != 'Port Scans'`,
    which is always true, so nothing was ever excluded. Titles listed in
    EXCLUDE_FINDING_TITLES (empty by default, to match the .docx output) are
    dropped here.
=========================================================================== -#}
{#- FINDINGS arrives in a different shape depending on which export path built
    the context: a list of groups each holding .FINDINGS (what the Word
    framework hands over), a flat list of findings, a dict keyed by finding id,
    or a dict keyed by severity/group holding the findings. Flatten all of them.

    Iterating a dict in Jinja yields its KEYS, which are strings, and `f.title`
    on a string quietly returns the built-in str.title method - truthy, so the
    key itself ends up in the list and every finding renders blank. Hence the
    `is mapping` guard before any attribute is read. -#}
{%- set report_findings = [] -%}
{%- set _entries = FINDINGS.values()|list if FINDINGS is mapping
             else (FINDINGS|list if FINDINGS and FINDINGS is not string else []) -%}
{%- for _entry in _entries -%}
  {%- set _grp = none -%}
  {%- if _entry is mapping -%}
    {%- set _grp = _entry.FINDINGS if _entry.FINDINGS else (_entry.findings if _entry.findings else none) -%}
  {%- elif _entry is sequence and _entry is not string -%}
    {#- a dict of severity -> [findings] -#}
    {%- set _grp = _entry -%}
  {%- endif -%}
  {%- if _grp -%}
    {%- for f in (_grp.values()|list if _grp is mapping else _grp) -%}
      {%- if f is mapping and f.title not in EXCLUDE_FINDING_TITLES -%}
        {%- set _ = report_findings.append(f) -%}
      {%- endif -%}
    {%- endfor -%}
  {%- elif _entry is mapping and (_entry.title or _entry.severity or _entry.description) -%}
    {%- if _entry.title not in EXCLUDE_FINDING_TITLES -%}
      {%- set _ = report_findings.append(_entry) -%}
    {%- endif -%}
  {%- endif -%}
{%- endfor -%}

{#- Status (Open / Closed) is printed on each finding only when at least one finding
    in the report is Closed in PlexTrac - a report where everything is still open
    (a first test, not a retest) shows no Status at all. Anything that is not Closed
    ("Open", "In Process", blank) prints as Open. -#}
{%- set bb.show_status = false -%}
{%- for f in report_findings -%}
  {%- if (f.status|default("", true)|string|trim|lower) == "closed" -%}
    {%- set bb.show_status = true -%}
  {%- endif -%}
{%- endfor -%}
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en-GB">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<title>{{ print_report("Report Title", "") }}{{ REPORT_TITLE_SUFFIX }}</title>
<style>
/* ==========================================================================
   Fonts. Static instances of DM Sans (the .docx asks for "DM Sans 14pt Light"
   and "DM Sans Medium") plus Archivo Medium for the section numbers.
   build/build.py rewrites these src: urls into base64 data: URIs.
   ========================================================================== */
@font-face { font-family: "DM Sans"; font-style: normal; font-weight: 300; src: url("data:font/woff2;base64,") format("woff2"); }
@font-face { font-family: "DM Sans"; font-style: normal; font-weight: 400; src: url("data:font/woff2;base64,") format("woff2"); }
@font-face { font-family: "DM Sans"; font-style: normal; font-weight: 500; src: url("data:font/woff2;base64,") format("woff2"); }
@font-face { font-family: "DM Sans"; font-style: normal; font-weight: 700; src: url("data:font/woff2;base64,") format("woff2"); }
@font-face { font-family: "DM Sans"; font-style: italic; font-weight: 300; src: url("data:font/woff2;base64,") format("woff2"); }
@font-face { font-family: "DM Sans"; font-style: italic; font-weight: 400; src: url("data:font/woff2;base64,") format("woff2"); }
@font-face { font-family: "Archivo"; font-style: normal; font-weight: 500; src: url("data:font/woff2;base64,") format("woff2"); }
@font-face { font-family: "DM Mono"; font-style: normal; font-weight: 400; src: url("data:font/woff2;base64,") format("woff2"); }

:root {
  /* Brand palette, lifted from the .docx */
  --brand:        #0049B8;   /* headings, footer                    */
  --accent:       #0089C2;   /* cover labels                        */
  --ink:          #242531;   /* body text (Normal style)            */
  --ink-table:    #252632;   /* table body text                     */
  --rule:         #99C2FF;   /* rating table body borders           */
  --rule-head:    #C2DAFF;   /* rating table header fill + borders  */
  --rule-grid:    #000000;   /* finding header table (Table Grid)   */

  /* Word "line rule auto" multiplies the font's natural line height.
     DM Sans is 1.32em, so a Word line value of N/240 becomes N/240 * 1.32. */
  --lh-body:      1.584;     /* w:line 288 */
  --lh-h1:        1.529;     /* w:line 278 */
  --lh-h2:        1.424;     /* w:line 259 */
  --lh-tight:     1.320;     /* w:line 240 */
  --lh-cover:     1.188;     /* w:line 216 */
  --lh-finding:   1.980;     /* w:line 360 */

  --sans: "DM Sans", "Segoe UI", "Helvetica Neue", Arial, sans-serif;
  --mono: "DM Mono", "Cascadia Mono", Consolas, "DejaVu Sans Mono", monospace;

  /* Space after a table = the empty paragraph the .docx puts there. */
  --after-finding-table: 17pt;   /* an empty "Heading 2 two" paragraph */
  --after-rating-table:  33.4pt; /* an empty "Normal" paragraph        */

  /* Gap between paragraphs inside a finding. The .docx placeholder paragraph
     is "space after 0, line 1.5", which governs the block as a whole; the
     paragraphs within it come from PlexTrac's rich text. 8pt keeps them
     distinguishable - set it to 0 to close the gap completely. */
  --finding-para-space: 8pt;

  /* Space after each finding = the empty paragraph inside the .docx loop. */
  --after-finding: 33.4pt;
}

/* ==========================================================================
   Page. A4, margins 2cm / 2cm / 2cm, footer in the bottom margin.
   (.docx w:pgSz 11900x16840, w:pgMar top/left/right 1133tw, bottom 850tw)
   ========================================================================== */
@page {
  size: A4;
  margin: 2cm 2cm 1.65cm 2cm;

  @bottom-left {
    content: "{{ CONFIDENTIALITY }}";
    font-family: "DM Sans", Arial, sans-serif; font-weight: 300; font-size: 9pt; color: #0049B8;
    vertical-align: top; margin: 0; padding: 0;
  }
  @bottom-center {
    content: "";
    width: 1.51cm; height: 0.88cm; vertical-align: top;
    background: url("data:image/png;base64,") no-repeat center top;
    background-size: contain;
  }
  @bottom-right {
    content: "Page " counter(page) " of " counter(pages);
    font-family: "DM Sans", Arial, sans-serif; font-weight: 300; font-size: 9pt; color: #0049B8;
    vertical-align: top; text-align: right; margin: 0; padding: 0;
  }
}

/* Title page: no margins (the owl bleeds off the right edge) and no footer,
   matching <w:titlePg/> in the .docx. */
@page :first {
  margin: 0;
  @bottom-left   { content: none; }
  @bottom-center { content: none; background: none; }
  @bottom-right  { content: none; }
}

/* ==========================================================================
   Base typography (Word "Normal": DM Sans Light 11pt, #242531, 16pt after)
   ========================================================================== */
body {
  margin: 0;
  font-family: var(--sans);
  font-weight: 300;
  font-size: 11pt;
  line-height: var(--lh-body);
  color: var(--ink);
  counter-reset: h1;
}

p { margin: 0 0 16pt; }
p.plain { white-space: pre-wrap; }
b, strong { font-weight: 500; }
a { color: var(--brand); text-decoration: none; }
img { max-width: 100%; }
code, kbd, samp, pre { font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 0.92em; }
pre {
  margin: 0 0 12pt; padding: 6pt 8pt; white-space: pre-wrap; word-wrap: break-word;
  background: #F4F7FD; border-left: 2pt solid var(--rule-head); line-height: 1.35;
}

/* A note the template emits when PlexTrac data is missing. */
.tpl-note { color: #B00020; }

/* DEBUG_CONTEXT output only. */
.debug { break-after: page; page-break-after: always; }
.debug-h1 { font-size: 18pt; font-weight: 500; color: var(--brand); margin: 0 0 10pt; }
.debug-h2 { font-size: 11pt; font-weight: 500; color: var(--brand); margin: 10pt 0 2pt; }
.debug pre {
  font-size: 7pt; line-height: 1.3; background: #F4F7FD;
  white-space: pre-wrap; word-break: break-all;
}

/* ==========================================================================
   Headings. Numbering is done with CSS counters so it survives any content.
   H1 "1.  Title"   - number Archivo Medium 16pt #242531, title DM Sans Medium 18pt #0049B8
   H2 "1.1 Title"   - all DM Sans Medium 14pt #0049B8
   H3               - Word style "Heading 2 two": DM Sans Medium 12pt, no number
   ========================================================================== */
h1, h2, h3 { font-weight: 500; color: var(--brand); break-after: avoid; page-break-after: avoid; }

h1 {
  counter-increment: h1; counter-reset: h2;
  font-size: 18pt; line-height: var(--lh-h1);
  margin: 0 0 24pt; padding: 6pt 0 0 0.75cm; text-indent: -0.75cm;
}
h1::before {
  content: counter(h1) ".";
  display: inline-block; width: 0.75cm; text-indent: 0;
  font-family: "Archivo", var(--sans); font-size: 16pt; color: var(--ink);
}

h2 {
  counter-increment: h2;
  font-size: 14pt; line-height: var(--lh-h2);
  margin: 0; padding-left: 0.75cm; text-indent: -0.75cm;
}
h2::before { content: counter(h1) "." counter(h2); display: inline-block; width: 0.75cm; text-indent: 0; }

h3 { font-size: 12pt; line-height: var(--lh-h2); margin: 0; }

/* Sections that carry <w:pageBreakBefore/> in the .docx. */
.new-page { break-before: page; page-break-before: always; }
{%- if PAGE_BREAK_PER_H1 %}
h1 { break-before: page; page-break-before: always; }
.cover + h1 { break-before: auto; page-break-before: auto; }
{%- endif %}

/* ==========================================================================
   Lists ("List Paragraph": 1.27cm indent, bullet at 0.62cm, contextual spacing)
   ========================================================================== */
ul, ol { margin: 0 0 16pt; padding: 0 0 0 1.27cm; }
li { margin: 0; }
/* Word's List Paragraph geometry - bullet at 0.62cm, text at 1.27cm - applied
   to every unordered list so PlexTrac rich text lines up with template lists. */
ul { list-style: none; padding: 0; }
ul > li { padding-left: 1.27cm; text-indent: -0.65cm; }
ul > li::before { content: "\2022"; display: inline-block; width: 0.65cm; text-indent: 0; }
ul ul, ol ul { margin: 0; padding-left: 1.27cm; }

/* ==========================================================================
   Cover page. Images are anchored to the page in the .docx (wrapNone), so they
   are positioned absolutely; the text below reproduces the Word paragraph flow.
   ========================================================================== */
.cover {
  position: relative;
  width: 21cm; height: 29.7cm;
  padding: 2cm 2cm 0;
  box-sizing: border-box;
  overflow: hidden;
  break-after: page; page-break-after: always;
}
.cover-logo { position: absolute; left: 1.84cm; top: 1.44cm; width: 3.89cm; height: 2.35cm; }
.cover-owl  { position: absolute; left: 12.87cm; top: 5.07cm; width: 8.16cm; height: 18.10cm; }
.cover-flow { position: relative; width: 12.6cm; }

/* Each rule below is one paragraph of the .docx cover, in document order.
   Word gives an empty paragraph a full line box; CSS gives it zero height, so
   the empty ones carry an explicit height of font-size x line-height. */
.cv-sp-48   { font-size: 48pt; line-height: var(--lh-cover); margin: 0; height: 57.02pt; }
.cv-sp-40   { font-size: 40pt; line-height: var(--lh-cover); margin: 0; height: 47.52pt; }
.cv-title   { font-size: 39pt; line-height: var(--lh-cover); margin: 0; color: var(--brand); font-weight: 300; }
.cv-sp-39   { font-size: 39pt; line-height: var(--lh-body); margin: 0; height: 61.78pt; padding-bottom: 16pt; }
.cv-sp-11   { font-size: 11pt; line-height: var(--lh-body); margin: 0; height: 17.42pt; padding-bottom: 16pt; }
.cv-version { font-size: 10pt; line-height: var(--lh-cover); margin: 0; color: var(--accent); }
.cv-date    { font-size: 12pt; line-height: var(--lh-body); margin: 0; padding-top: 4pt; }
.cv-sp-12   { font-size: 12pt; line-height: var(--lh-body); margin: 0; height: 19.01pt; padding-top: 4pt; }
.cv-prep    { font-size: 10pt; line-height: var(--lh-tight); margin: 0; color: var(--accent); }
.cv-author  { font-size: 12pt; line-height: var(--lh-body); margin: 0; padding-top: 10pt; }
.cv-email   { font-size: 11pt; line-height: var(--lh-body); margin: 0; }
/* with no author name, the email takes over the name's slot under "Prepared by:" */
.cv-prep + .cv-email { padding-top: 10pt; }
.cv-sp-18   { font-size: 18pt; line-height: var(--lh-body); margin: 0; height: 28.51pt; padding-bottom: 16pt; }
.cover-crest { display: block; width: 5.58cm; height: 2.63cm; }

/* ==========================================================================
   Findings
   ========================================================================== */
{%- if PAGE_BREAK_PER_FINDING %}
.finding { break-before: page; page-break-before: always; }
/* the first finding stays with the "Findings" heading - :first-of-type would
   match nothing here, since the first <section> on the page is the cover */
h1 + .finding { break-before: auto; page-break-before: auto; }
{%- endif %}

/* The .docx puts the title and severity in a 2-column Table Grid,
   10206tw wide (18cm), split 7371tw / 2835tw. */
table.finding-head {
  width: 18cm; table-layout: fixed; border-collapse: collapse;
  margin: 0 0 var(--after-finding-table);
  break-inside: avoid; page-break-inside: avoid; break-after: avoid;
}
table.finding-head td {
  border: 0.5pt solid var(--rule-grid);
  vertical-align: middle; padding: 0 0.19cm;
}
td.finding-title { width: 13cm; }
td.finding-title h2 { margin: 0; }
td.finding-sev {
  width: 5cm; text-align: center;
  font-size: 30pt; line-height: var(--lh-h1); color: var(--brand);
}

/* Finding body: 10pt, 1.5 line spacing. The header table is excluded so its
   title and severity keep their own sizes. */
.finding { margin: 0 0 var(--after-finding); }
.finding p, .finding li, .finding div,
.finding table:not(.finding-head) td, .finding table:not(.finding-head) th {
  font-size: 10pt; line-height: var(--lh-finding);
}
.finding p { margin: 0 0 var(--finding-para-space); }
.finding ul, .finding ol, .finding table, .finding pre, .finding blockquote, .finding figure {
  margin: 0 0 10pt;
}
.finding h3 { break-after: avoid; page-break-after: avoid; }

/* Affected assets list: 10pt, 6pt above and below the block. */
ul.assets { margin: 6pt 0; }
.finding ul.assets > li { line-height: var(--lh-tight); }

/* Status: Open / Closed, only on a report where something has been closed. */
.finding p.status { margin: 0 0 var(--finding-para-space); }
.status-value { font-weight: 500; color: var(--brand); }

/* CVSS score: the score in the brand colour, the vector under it in mono. */
.finding p.cvss { margin: 0 0 var(--finding-para-space); }
.cvss-score { font-weight: 500; color: var(--brand); }
.cvss-vector { font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 9pt; overflow-wrap: anywhere; }

/* Screenshots pasted into a write-up: never wider than the text, never distorted,
   never split across a page. */
.finding img { height: auto; }
.finding figure.image, .finding img { break-inside: avoid; page-break-inside: avoid; }

figure.exhibit { break-inside: avoid; page-break-inside: avoid; text-align: center; }
figure.exhibit img { max-width: 100%; }
figure.exhibit figcaption { font-size: 9pt; color: var(--ink-table); padding-top: 4pt; }

/* ==========================================================================
   Risk rating table (9629tw wide = 17cm, split 1833tw / 7796tw)
   ========================================================================== */
table.rating {
  width: 17cm; table-layout: fixed; border-collapse: collapse;
  margin: 0 0 var(--after-rating-table);
  color: var(--ink-table);
}
table.rating col.sev { width: 3.23cm; }
table.rating th, table.rating td {
  border: 1pt solid var(--rule);
  padding: 6pt 0.1cm 6pt 0.35cm;
  line-height: var(--lh-tight);
  text-align: left;
}
table.rating thead th {
  background: var(--rule-head); border-color: var(--rule-head);
  color: var(--brand); font-weight: 500; font-size: 11pt;
}
table.rating thead th + th { border-left-color: #FFFFFF; }
table.rating tbody tr:first-child td { border-top-color: var(--rule-head); }
table.rating td.sev { vertical-align: middle; font-size: 11pt; }
table.rating td.desc { font-size: 10pt; }
table.rating tr { break-inside: avoid; page-break-inside: avoid; }

/* Rich text arriving from PlexTrac ------------------------------------- */
.finding table:not(.finding-head) { border-collapse: collapse; }
.finding table:not(.finding-head) td, .finding table:not(.finding-head) th {
  border: 0.5pt solid var(--rule); padding: 3pt 5pt; text-align: left;
}
blockquote { margin: 0 0 12pt; padding-left: 10pt; border-left: 2pt solid var(--rule-head); }
</style>
</head>
<body>

{#- ========================= CONTEXT DIAGNOSTIC ============================
    Set DEBUG_CONTEXT = true at the top of this file, rebuild, upload and
    export once. The first pages of the PDF then show exactly what PlexTrac
    handed the template, which is the fastest way to find a field that is not
    coming through. Set it back to false afterwards.
=========================================================================== -#}
{%- if DEBUG_CONTEXT %}
<section class="debug">
  <p class="debug-h1">Template context</p>

  <p class="debug-h2">Which top-level names exist</p>
  <pre>REPORT_INFO      {{ "yes" if _had.REPORT_INFO else "no" }}
CLIENT_INFO      {{ "yes" if _had.CLIENT_INFO else "no" }}
FINDINGS         {{ "yes" if _had.FINDINGS else "no" }}
FINDING_SUMMARY  {{ "yes" if _had.FINDING_SUMMARY else "no" }}
report           {{ "yes" if report is defined else "no" }}
client           {{ "yes" if client is defined else "no" }}
findings         {{ "yes" if findings is defined else "no" }}
finding_summary  {{ "yes" if finding_summary is defined else "no" }}
REPORT           {{ "yes" if REPORT is defined else "no" }}
CLIENT           {{ "yes" if CLIENT is defined else "no" }}
NARRATIVES       {{ "yes" if NARRATIVES is defined else "no" }}
data             {{ "yes" if data is defined else "no" }}</pre>

  <p class="debug-h2">Narratives found: {{ narratives|length }}</p>
  <pre>{% for field in narratives %}label = {{ _label(field)|e }}
{% else %}none - display_narrative() will print the missing-narrative note for every section
{% endfor %}</pre>

  <p class="debug-h2">Findings collected: {{ report_findings|length }}</p>
  <pre>FINDINGS is a {{ "mapping" if FINDINGS is mapping else ("string" if FINDINGS is string else ("sequence" if FINDINGS is sequence else "scalar")) }} of {{ FINDINGS|length }}
{% if FINDINGS is mapping %}keys: {{ FINDINGS.keys()|list|join(", ")|e|truncate(400, true) }}
{% endif %}{% for f in report_findings %}{{ f.title|e }} [{{ f.severity|e }}]
{% else %}none
{% endfor %}</pre>

  <p class="debug-h2">REPORT_INFO</p>
  <pre>{{ REPORT_INFO|pprint|e|truncate(24000, true, "  ...[truncated]") }}</pre>

  <p class="debug-h2">CLIENT_INFO</p>
  <pre>{{ CLIENT_INFO|pprint|e|truncate(6000, true, "  ...[truncated]") }}</pre>

  <p class="debug-h2">FINDING_SUMMARY</p>
  <pre>{{ FINDING_SUMMARY|pprint|e|truncate(4000, true, "  ...[truncated]") }}</pre>

  <p class="debug-h2">First collected finding</p>
  <pre>{{ (report_findings[0] if report_findings else "none collected")|pprint|e|truncate(16000, true, "  ...[truncated]") }}</pre>

  <p class="debug-h2">FINDINGS (first entry, before flattening)</p>
  <pre>{{ (_entries[0] if _entries else "empty")|pprint|e|truncate(8000, true, "  ...[truncated]") }}</pre>
</section>
{% endif %}

{#- =============================== COVER ================================== -#}
<section class="cover">
  <img class="cover-logo" src="data:image/png;base64," alt="Cognisys">
  <img class="cover-owl" src="data:image/png;base64," alt="">
  <div class="cover-flow">
    <p class="cv-sp-48"></p>
    <p class="cv-sp-40"></p>
    <p class="cv-title">{{ print_report("Report Title", "") }}{{ REPORT_TITLE_SUFFIX }}</p>
    <p class="cv-sp-39"></p>
    <p class="cv-sp-11"></p>
    <p class="cv-sp-11"></p>
    <p class="cv-sp-11"></p>
    <p class="cv-sp-11"></p>
    <p class="cv-version">Version {{ print_report("Version", REPORT_VERSION) }}</p>
    <p class="cv-date">{{ export_date }}</p>
{%- set has_author = check_report("Author 1")|trim %}
{%- set has_author_email = check_report("Author 1 Email")|trim %}
{%- if has_author or has_author_email %}
    <p class="cv-sp-12"></p>
    <p class="cv-prep">Prepared by:</p>
    {%- if has_author %}
    <p class="cv-author">{{ print_report("Author 1") }}</p>
    {%- endif %}
    {%- if has_author_email %}
    <p class="cv-email">{{ print_report("Author 1 Email") }}</p>
    {%- endif %}
{%- endif %}
    <p class="cv-sp-18"></p>
    <img class="cover-crest" src="data:image/png;base64," alt="CREST Penetration Testing and OVS accredited">
  </div>
</section>

{#- ============================ INTRODUCTION =============================== -#}
<h1>Introduction</h1>

<h2>Overview</h2>
{{ display_narrative("Overview") }}

<h2>Scope</h2>
{{ display_narrative("Scope") }}

<h2>Caveats</h2>
{{ display_narrative("Limitation") }}

<h2>Disclaimer</h2>
{{ display_narrative("Disclaimer") }}

<h2>Confidentiality Notice</h2>
{{ display_narrative("Confidentiality Notice") }}

{#- ========================== EXECUTIVE SUMMARY ============================ -#}
<h1>Executive Summary</h1>
{{ display_narrative("Executive Summary") }}

{#- =============================== ROADMAP ================================= -#}
<h1 class="new-page">Roadmap</h1>
{{ display_narrative("Roadmap") }}

{#- ============================= ISSUE MATRIX ============================== -#}
<h1>Issue Matrix</h1>
<p>The following section lists all issues identified during this assessment, their severity rating, and a brief description, along with the associated IDs:</p>
{{ display_narrative("Issue Matrix") }}

{#- ============================== METHODOLOGY ============================== -#}
<h1 class="new-page">Methodology</h1>
{{ display_narrative("Methodology") }}

{#- ================================ FINDINGS ===============================
    CVSS Score and Affected Assets are printed only when the finding has them in
    PlexTrac: a finding with no score, or no assets, shows no heading for them. -#}
<h1>Findings</h1>
{% for f in report_findings %}
<section class="finding">
  <table class="finding-head">
    <tr>
      <td class="finding-title"><h2>{{ f.title|e }}</h2></td>
      <td class="finding-sev">{{ f.severity|replace("rmational", "")|e }}</td>
    </tr>
  </table>

  {%- if bb.show_status %}
  <h3>Status</h3>
  <p class="status"><span class="status-value">{{ "Closed" if (f.status|default("", true)|string|trim|lower) == "closed" else "Open" }}</span></p>
  {%- endif %}

  {#- CVSS score, newest CVSS version first. A version counts as set when it has a
      vector or a score above 0.0 - an untouched PlexTrac calculator has neither. -#}
  {%- set ss.cvss_key = "" -%}
  {%- for _k in ["CVSS4", "CVSS3_1", "CVSS3", "CVSS2"] -%}
    {%- set _s = f.risk_score[_k] if f.risk_score is mapping and f.risk_score[_k] is mapping else {} -%}
    {%- if not ss.cvss_key and (_s.vector|default("", true)|string|trim or (_s.overall|default(-1)|float(-1)) > 0) -%}
      {%- set ss.cvss_key = _k -%}
    {%- endif -%}
  {%- endfor -%}
  {%- if ss.cvss_key %}
  {%- set _s = f.risk_score[ss.cvss_key] %}
  <h3>CVSS Score</h3>
  <p class="cvss">
    {%- if (_s.overall|default(-1)|float(-1)) >= 0 %}<span class="cvss-score">{{ "%.1f"|format(_s.overall|default(0)|float(0)) }}</span> {% endif -%}
    ({{ {"CVSS4": "CVSS 4.0", "CVSS3_1": "CVSS 3.1", "CVSS3": "CVSS 3.0", "CVSS2": "CVSS 2.0"}[ss.cvss_key] }})
    {%- if _s.vector|default("", true)|string|trim %}<br><span class="cvss-vector">{{ _s.vector|string|trim|e }}</span>{% endif -%}
  </p>
  {%- endif %}

  <h3>Summary</h3>
  {{ rich(f.description) }}

  <h3>Proof of Concept</h3>
  {{ print_finding(f, 'proof_of_concept') }}
  {{ print_finding(f, 'technical_details') }}

  <h3>Recommendation</h3>
  {{ print_finding(f, 'recommendations') }}

  {#- Affected assets: one line per asset, or per asset:port where PlexTrac lists
      ports. Collected first, so a finding whose assets are all blank prints no
      heading. PlexTrac names an asset in `asset`; `name` is the older shape. The
      .docx printed data.number for the port, which is never set on the asset; the
      port's own number is what was meant. -#}
  {%- set _assets = f.assets if f.assets else f.affected_assets -%}
  {%- set asset_lines = [] -%}
  {%- for data in (_assets.values()|list if _assets is mapping else (_assets if _assets and _assets is not string else [])) -%}
    {%- if data is mapping -%}
      {%- set ss.asset = (data.asset if data.asset else (data.name if data.name else ""))|string|trim -%}
      {%- if ss.asset -%}
        {%- set _ports = data.ports.items()|list if data.ports is mapping else [] -%}
        {%- for port_id, port_data in _ports -%}
          {%- set ss.port = (port_data.number if port_data is mapping and port_data.number else port_id)|string|trim -%}
          {%- set _ = asset_lines.append(ss.asset ~ (":" ~ ss.port if ss.port else "")) -%}
        {%- else -%}
          {%- set _ = asset_lines.append(ss.asset) -%}
        {%- endfor -%}
      {%- endif -%}
    {%- elif data is string and data|trim -%}
      {%- set _ = asset_lines.append(data|trim) -%}
    {%- endif -%}
  {%- endfor -%}
  {%- if asset_lines %}
  <h3>Affected Assets</h3>
  <ul class="bullets assets">
    {%- for line in asset_lines|unique %}
    <li>{{ line|e }}</li>
    {%- endfor %}
  </ul>
  {%- endif %}

  {%- if check_finding(f, 'further_reading')|trim %}
  <h3>Further Reading</h3>
  {{ print_finding(f, 'further_reading') }}
  {%- endif %}

  {%- if SHOW_EXHIBITS and f.exhibits %}
  <h3>Screenshots</h3>
  {{ print_screenshots(f) }}
  {%- endif %}
</section>
{% endfor %}

{#- ========================= RISK RATING EXPLANATION ======================= -#}
<h1 class="new-page">Risk Rating Explanation</h1>

<h2>Overview</h2>
<p>Throughout this document, each vulnerability or risk identified has been labelled and assigned a reference number. Risk ratings have also been applied and fall into one of five categories: Critical, High, Medium, Low, and Informational. The definition for these categories is as follows:</p>

<table class="rating">
  <colgroup><col class="sev"><col></colgroup>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Severity Levels</th><th>Description</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td class="sev">Critical</td>
      <td class="desc">This classification describes those vulnerabilities that can directly lead to host compromise, unauthorised access to sensitive data, or significant reputational and financial damage.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td class="sev">High</td>
      <td class="desc">This classification of vulnerability presents a severe threat to the operational performance or integrity of the assessed hosts. The exploitation of such vulnerabilities can lead to the compromise of hosts (or host data), but typically only as a blended threat or utilising user coercion.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td class="sev">Medium</td>
      <td class="desc">This describes vulnerabilities that may present an attacker with limited host data or access, usually reliant upon user coercion or specific requirements to be in place. The exploitation of such an issue may cause reputational damage, however, the likelihood of this occurring is moderate.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td class="sev">Low</td>
      <td class="desc">Vulnerabilities in this category may present an attacker with information regarding the supporting architecture, minor data leakage, or contact information of internal staff. These issues would generally have a limited impact on the security posture of an organisation.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td class="sev">Informational</td>
      <td class="desc">Observations are points worthy of note but do not necessarily present a direct risk to the data, host, or application.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2>Likelihood / Impact</h2>
<p>The overall risk severity detailed above is the aggregate of the following elements:</p>
<ul class="bullets">
  <li>Likelihood / Ease of exploitation - Likelihood is how probable it is that an event will occur. For example, a cyber breach seems very likely to occur when there are no firewalls, anti-virus software, or intrusion detection software to prevent it.</li>
  <li>Impact of the associated attack - The impact is an estimate of the harm that could be caused by an event. For example, a ransomware attack could have a catastrophic impact, whereas an out-of-date JavaScript library may have a low impact.</li>
</ul>

</body>
</html>
